# CODEOWNERS
#
# Requires review from the listed owner(s) before a PR touching these paths
# can be merged, assuming branch protection has "Require review from Code
# Owners" enabled for the target branch (Settings → Branches → Branch
# protection rules).
#
# Replace @your-github-username / @your-org/team-name below with real
# GitHub usernames or team handles before this has any effect — GitHub
# ignores CODEOWNERS entries that don't resolve to a valid user/team with
# repo access.

# Default owner for anything not matched more specifically below.
*                                   @your-org/maintainers

# --- CI / CD pipeline definitions ---
# Changes here can silently weaken or disable security gates (e.g. removing
# a `permissions:` block, dropping `--error` from a scanner, changing
# `exit-code` on a vulnerability scan from failing to non-failing). Treat
# any diff to these files as security-relevant even when it looks like
# routine CI maintenance.
/.github/workflows/                @your-org/security @your-org/maintainers
/.github/CODEOWNERS                @your-org/security @your-org/maintainers
/.github/owasp-suppressions.xml    @your-org/security

# --- Containers & deployment ---
# Dockerfile changes affect what's baked into the production image (base
# image version, installed packages, user/permissions, COPY sources).
/backend/Dockerfile                @your-org/security @your-org/maintainers
/frontend/Dockerfile               @your-org/security @your-org/maintainers
/docker-compose.yml                @your-org/maintainers
/k8s/                              @your-org/maintainers

# --- Auth, security middleware, and secrets-adjacent code ---
/backend/auth.py                   @your-org/security
/backend/security.py               @your-org/security
/backend/validators.py             @your-org/security
/env.example                       @your-org/security

# --- Dependency manifests ---
# A bumped or newly-added dependency is exactly what the dependency-audit
# jobs in security.yml exist to catch, but a human should still look at
# *why* something changed here.
/backend/requirements.txt          @your-org/security
/frontend/package.json             @your-org/security
/frontend/package-lock.json        @your-org/security
